 "sources": [
  "University of Kentucky Entomology, Bed Bugs (ENTFACT-636, M. Potter): adults about 3/16 inch, reddish-brown, oval and flattened; fit into crevices no wider than a toothpick; active mainly at night; the bite is painless and a blood meal takes about three to ten minutes; nymphs and adults persist months without feeding, up to about a year at 55 F or cooler; females lay one or more eggs a day, hundreds in a lifetime; eggs about the size of a dust speck; nymphs molt five times and need a blood meal between each molt; disease transmission to humans not proven and considered unlikely; hide in mattress seams, box springs, bed frames, headboards; https://entomology.mgcafe.uky.edu/ef636",
  "US EPA, Bed Bugs: Appearance and Life Cycle: adult about the size of an apple seed; https://www.epa.gov/bedbugs/bed-bugs-appearance-and-life-cycle",
  "US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, About Bed Bugs: 1-7 mm; not known to spread disease; bite at night while people sleep; stay near where people sleep; spread by hiding in luggage, bags, folded clothes and furniture; https://www.cdc.gov/bed-bugs/about/index.html",
  "University of Kentucky College of Agriculture news, UK entomologists help sequence bed bug genome: traumatic insemination, 'a male pierces the female's body and deposits sperm into her body cavity'; https://news.mgcafe.uky.edu/article/uk-entomologists-help-sequence-bed-bug-genome",
  "Wang et al. 2009, Journal of Economic Entomology (hosted by Purdue Extension), bed bug pitfall traps: carbon dioxide and heat both attract bed bugs, CO2 more strongly; https://www.extension.entm.purdue.edu/bedbugs/pdf/BedBugTrap.pdf"
 ],
 "beats": [
  {
   "m": "name",
   "say": "Number six. The bed bug. Flat, reddish brown, about the size of an apple seed."
  },
  {
   "m": "fact",
   "say": "By day, it hides in the seams of your mattress."
  },
  {
   "m": "fact",
   "say": "At night, it follows your breath. The carbon dioxide you breathe out."
  },
  {
   "m": "escalate",
   "say": "Then it bites, and drinks your blood for up to ten minutes. And you never feel a thing."
  },
  {
   "m": "fact",
   "say": "The good news? They're not known to spread disease. Just itchy bites."
  },
